Virtual mallocng grows an individually mmapped chunk in place

musl grows a large chunk with mremap, which moves its page-table entries
and copies nothing. The capability heap has no mremap: patch 0009's
realloc takes its remap branch only when the chunk's address can be
published at the new size, and __heap_remapping then maps, copies and
unmaps anyway. So every large growth copied the whole old object, and on
the native route the copy also read, and so pinned, every untouched page
of the old mapping. 505.mcf_r's two resizes copied 235 MB and 459 MB arc
arrays twice each: 230k of the test run's 395k pinned pages, where
riscv-base's musl moves them for nothing.

A growing mmapped chunk now stays where it is when its mapping has the
room and its address is aligned for the new size: a fresh child for the
new size, the old child ended, the in-place branch's bookkeeping. When
it must move, it moves to a mapping with room for twice the new size
(up to REALLOC_ROOM_MAX), aligned for that capacity, so the next growth
stays in place: one copy of the old size, as with doubling. The room is
address space until touched, because a native arena pins a page at its
first access. Shrinking and small chunks are unchanged.

